WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.100
This query creates one summary row for each
diagnosis That sentence tells

00:00:05.125 --> 00:00:09.000
us the grain before we even read the SQL The SELECT
list

00:00:09.025 --> 00:00:13.162
contains diagnosis which is the grouping column and
COUNT star which counts

00:00:13.188 --> 00:00:18.725
the admission rows inside each diagnosis group The
alias admission_count gives the

00:00:18.738 --> 00:00:23.950
measure a clear name GROUP BY diagnosis tells
PostgreSQL to collect rows

00:00:23.975 --> 00:00:28.950
that share the same diagnosis Then COUNT star is
calculated separately for

00:00:28.975 --> 00:00:33.888
each group The ORDER BY clause sorts by
admission_count descending so the

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:39.275
most frequent diagnosis appears first Diagnosis is
used as a secondary sort

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.425
so groups with the same count still have a
predictable order Run

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:47.738
the query and look at the result Each diagnosis
should appear once

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:52.100
even if several source rows had that diagnosis That
is the key

00:00:52.125 --> 00:00:55.638
effect of grouping many detail rows become one
summary row for the

00:00:55.662 --> 00:01:00.350
defined grain If you are ever unsure about a grouped
query temporarily

00:01:00.375 --> 00:01:05.138
inspect the detail rows for one category Count them
manually and compare

00:01:05.162 --> 00:01:10.325
with the aggregate result That validation technique
is simple and very effective
